\Babstract{
This paper proposes removal of digraphs.
}

\make_contents 

\h2(listed=false){Revision history}

\h3{R1 October 2026}
\ul{
  \li{Redid iconv analysis as there were some issues with deduplication of encodings in the previous version}
  \li{Added released standards and a reference to N033 to the timeline}
  \li{Clarified _Pragma usability and other alternatives}
}

\h3{R0 September 2026}

Original version of the paper.

\h2{Introduction}

Digraphs are a complicated solution to a very old problem, which cause more problems than they solve in a modern environment. 
Digraphs also severely limit the design space of C++, although as we have seen with \ref("P2996") we are already fine with special-casing our way out of this pickle.

This however leads to a rather unfortunate situation. If you need to use a source encoding that requires use of digraphs, you \b{cannot use all of C++26} directly, resulting in a fragmented and needlessly complex language. 
Some parts you can use if you require certain digraphs, some you don't.

Since we are most likely going to continue seeing similar problems, this paper proposes to remove the digraphs \tcode{<%}, \tcode{%>}, \tcode{<:}, \tcode{:>}, \tcode{%:} and \tcode{%:%:} from the language entirely. 
This does not affect the alternative keyword tokens (\tcode{and}, \tcode{or}, etc.).


\h2{History}
The \ref("https://www.gnu.org/software/c-intro-and-ref/manual/html_node/Digraphs.html"){GCC documentation} notes:
\Bquote{
Apparently in the 1990s some computer systems had trouble inputting these characters, or trouble displaying them. These digraphs almost never appear in C programs nowadays, but we mention them for completeness. 
}

What this refers to is systems that require the source encoding to be something that does not have the characters \tcode{#}, \tcode{[}, \tcode{]}, \tcode{\{} or \tcode{\}}. In such cases, the corresponding digraphs are required to write those characters in source code.

Such encodings are rather uncommon nowadays, but they do exist.

Historically digraphs were introduced because of \ref("ISO 646"), which includes several national variants that do not have square brackets or curly braces. For example, the \ref("ISO-IR-021") (also known as DIN 66003) variant used in Germany replaces \tcode{[} with \tcode{Ä}, \tcode{]} with \tcode{Ü}, \tcode{\{} with \tcode{ä} and \tcode{\}} with \tcode{ü}.

Keld Simonsen pointed out this problem with some ISO 646 variants including the Danish \ref("ISO-646-DK") in a 1984 usenet post.
To address this issue, he suggested the following digraphs as a solution (\ref("Simonsen, 1984")):
\Bquote{
\digraphs(columns=3){
  \digraph("(.", "{")
  \digraph(").", "}")
  \digraph("(*", "[")
  \digraph(")*", "]")
  \digraph("!.", "|")
  \digraph("!*", "\\")
}
}



Even then Martin Minow argued against such a solution, as it was a vanishing problem even at the time. 

To quote \ref("Minow, 1984"):
\Bquote{
There isn't any really good solution --
it is highly unlikely that the C standardization committee will
remove these characters from the language.  While most of them
can be replaced by suitable #defines, several cannot, notably
backslash.  The only short-term solution would be for the
parties affected to write NRC-specific pre-processors.

In the long term, however, the problem will go away as people
move to an 8-bit character set such as Dec-Multinational or
the pending ISO standard that is almost identical to it.
}

That "pending ISO standard" Minow mentions later became \ref("ISO/IEC 8859"). 
The relevant part of ISO/IEC 8859 for the Danish language is ISO/IEC 8859-1, which was first published in March 1985 as \ref("ECMA-94").

ECMA-94 fully solves the described issue by using the 8th bit in an 8-bit byte to allow for another 96 printable characters, which includes everything that was potentially missing in some ISO 646 variants.

Interestingly, \ref("ISO-IR-027") even lacks \tcode{:}, making four of the digraphs we eventually ended up with unusable. 
While it is explicitly based on ISO 646, ISO-IR-027 is not a true variant - some of the added letters are mapped to code points which are invariant in ISO 646. 
However, this shows that even for its original purpose, digraphs are more of a band-aid than a real solution.

\h3{Timeline}
So, how did we get here? Unfortunately not a lot of documents from the early days survived. However, the publicly available X3J11, X3J16, WG14 and WG21 documents allow reconstructing roughly this timeline (please reach out if you have corrections or additional information!):

\timeline{

  \timeline_year(year="1984"){
    \timeline_event(
      date="August 27, 1984",
      title="C and ANSI Standard",
      source={\ref("Simonsen, 1984")}
    ){
      In a Usenet post, Keld Simonsen suggests digraphs as a solution to the problem of missing characters in some ISO 646 variants.
      \Bquote{
        Proposed digraphs:
        \digraphs(columns=3){
          \digraph("(.", "{")
          \digraph(").", "}")
          \digraph("(*", "[")
          \digraph(")*", "]")
          \digraph("!.", "|")
          \digraph("!*", "\\")
        }
        }
    }
  }
  \timeline_year(year="1985"){
    \timeline_event(
      date="March, 1985",
      title="ECMA-94 is published",
      source={\ref("ECMA-94")}
    ){
      ISO/IEC 8859-1 is published as ECMA-94, providing a solution for the problem of missing characters
    }
    \timeline_event(
      date="1985~1986",
      title="ANSI C introduces trigraphs",
      source={\ref("BYTE 1986") page 138 (146 in the PDF)}
    ){
      Trigraphs have been added to the X3J11 ANSI C standard. 
      
      Unfortunately no minutes of the actual meeting where it was voted in seem to have survived. However, the March edition of BYTE magazine notes:
\Bquote{
To make C easier to use internationally and to promote a wider use of C as an international language, the committee chose to address the problem of the "overused" ASCII character set within the Information Bulletin by defining a group of trigraph character sequences to act as operator equivalents.
}
    }
  }
  \timeline_year(year="1987"){
    \timeline_event(
      date="October 15, 1987",
      title="Tøndering, Alternative to Trigraphs",
    ){
      A WG14 proposal for digraphs as alternative to trigraphs is published by Tøndering. 

      WG14's \ref("https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/wg14_document_log.htm"){document log} lists this document, however the paper itself does not seem to have survived.

      However, its contents are quoted in \ref("WG14/N033") on page 2.
    }
    \timeline_event(
      date="December 11, 1987",
      title="X3J11 Meeting No. 19",
      source={\ref("X3J11/87-249") page 21}
    ){
      X3J11 votes against a motion to solve trigraph readability.
      \Bquote{
        Plauger presented a request by Tøndering (87-223, 87-224, 87-248) that we add digraphs for braces and brackets, and other readability aids to avoid most uses for trigraphs.

\codeblock("text"){
Straw vote:
  4 need to solve trigraph readability problem
  lots no
}
      }
    }
  }
  \timeline_year(year="1988"){
    \timeline_event(
      date="June 13, 1988",
      title="Simonsen and Stroustrup, A European Representation for ISO C (Revised N023)",
      source={\ref("WG14/N052")}
    ){
      A paper proposing digraphs is published by Simonsen and Stroustrup. The relevant proposed alternative tokens in this paper are:
      \Bquote{
        \digraphs(columns=3){
          \digraph("(:", "{")
          \digraph(":)", "}")
          \digraph("!(", "[")
          \digraph(")", "]")
          \digraph("??/", "\\")
        }}
        Note that \tcode{)} for \tcode{]} is not a typo. Here's a code example from the paper:
\Bquote{\codeblock("cpp"){
  main(argc, argv) char* argv!();
  (:
    if (argc<1 cor *argv!(1)=='??/0') return;
    printf("Hello,%s??/n", argv!(1));
  :)
}}
        The paper also discusses using an infix notation \tcode{argv!a} to spell \tcode{argv[a]}. 
        Also note that the paper argues that it should \i{not} be legal to mix these digraph constructs with the tokens they replace.
    }
    \timeline_event(
      date="August 19, 1988",
      title="Danish Comments on ISO/DP 9899-C",
      source={\ref("ISO/IEC JTC1/SC22/N528")}
    ){
      The Danish NB clarifies that reason for their \b{"no"} vote on DP 9899 is the remaining problem with regards to representation in non-ASCII ISO 646 character sets.
      
      Attached is another early version of the Danish digraph proposal. In this version the following replacements are proposed:
      \Bquote{
        \digraphs(columns=3){
          \digraph("(:", "{")
          \digraph(":)", "}")
          \digraph("a!b", "a[b]")
        }
      }

      This document also notes:
      \Bquote{
        The new constructs have been implemented as an undocumented feature in the C++ compiler by Bjarne Stroustrup, AT&T Bell Laboratories.
      }
    }

    \timeline_event(
      date="October 27, 1988",
      title="X3J11 Meeting No. 21",
      source={\ref("X3J11/88-155") pages 4-6}
    ){
      Plauger reports that Denmark objected strongly to the failure of X3J11 to adopt any form of digraphs and that he is authorized to submit the final X3J11 draft directly for registration as a DIS only if both the UK and Denmark NBs do not object to its content.

      Several arguments against the digraph proposal are raised, notably:
      \Bquote{
        Plum observed that people were at liberty to define various kinds of macros to change the syntactic sugar that sweetens C. He felt that X3J11 should take no stand on a particular set. He also observed that the proposed extensions did not solve the problem of representing punctuation readably within string literals, and do not solve the problem of writing declarations such as \tcode{a[]} (since \tcode{x!()} is presumably invalid.)

        Plum asked that X3J11 respectfully say no to the request from WG14. He also urged the Danish members of WG14 to reconsider their opposition to the draft as it stands.
      }

      \Bquote{
        Gwyn felt that the problem as presented was a red herring. He stated that all European shops that he knew had found various ways to deal with the presentation problem in ISO 646, and that these solutions did not belong in the standard.
      }

      Finally, a poll was taken:
\codeblock("text"){
Straw vote:
    0 accept digraphs a la 88-134
   40 no
}
      Due to this poll outcome digraphs were not adopted for C89.
    }
  }
  \timeline_year(year="1989"){
    \timeline_event(date="December, 1989", title="C89 is published"){
      In this version of C trigraphs are supported but digraphs are not.
    }
  }
  \timeline_year(year="1990"){
    \timeline_event(
      date="November 26, 1990",
      title="A European Representation for ISO C",
    ){
      Bjarne Stroustrup publishes a paper entitled "A European Representation for ISO C" as WG14/N141. Unfortunately this paper is not archived.
    }
  }

    \timeline_year(year="1991"){
    \timeline_event(
      date="May 13-15, 1991",
      title="WG14 Meeting in Tokyo, Japan",
      source={\ref("WG14/N173") pages 6-8}
    ){
      Lots of discussion around digraphs, mostly about in which lexing phase they should be dealt with. WG14 endorses the digraph proposal.
\codeblock("text"){
*** Resolution #4: WG14 endorses the digraph proposal from Denmark
(E-mail WG14.109 page 3) that involves the addition of five alternate
spellings <%, %>, <:, :>, and %% for {, }, [,], and #, respectively,
and the new header <iso646.h>. [Implicit in this was that these new
spellings be recognized in translation phase 5 NOT phase 1.]
}
    }

    \timeline_event(
      date="June 17-21, 1991",
      title="X3J16 Meeting No. 6",
      source={\ref("WG21/N0013") pages 7 and 14}
    ){
      At this meeting digraphs are formally adopted into C++.

      This is also the first meeting at which concerns about the \tcode{<:} digraph are raised. To quote the minutes:
      \Bquote{
        Charney expressed concern about using the \tcode{<:} digraph in C++ because of the \tcode{::} operator. 
        Plum said that Stroustrup long ago said that C++ programmers won't mind adding a space before \tcode{::} now and then.
      }
      More on this later.

      The following digraphs are proposed:
      \Bquote{
        \digraphs(columns=3){
          \digraph("<%", "{")
          \digraph("%>", "}")
          \digraph("<:", "[")
          \digraph(":>", "]")
          \digraph("%%", "#")
        }
      }

      Two polls were taken:
\codeblock("text"){
  1. Add these names and digraphs to C++ as alternate spellings for their
     corresponding tokens.

     Motion passed: 24 yes, 2 no.

  2. Ask WG14 to consider replacing not_eq with <>.
     
     Motion passed: 22 yes, 2 no.
}
    }
  }
  \timeline_year(year="1992"){
    \timeline_event(
      date="May 13-15, 1992",
      title="WG14 Meeting in Salt Lake City, USA",
      source={\ref("WG14/N224")}
    ){
      Digraphs and the requests from WG21 are discussed again. 

      Notably, the digraph \tcode{<>} was pointed out as superfluous and conflicting with another X3J11.1 proposal and may cause problems for other extensions. 
      Similar issues are raised about \tcode{%%}, which additionally seemed to conflict with the language C*.
      It's also pointed out that WG21 failed to include \tcode{%%%%} as a replacement for \tcode{##}.

      Some noteworthy comments include:
      \Bquote{
        Gwyn objected (yet again) that the entire digraph approach was an attempt to solve a vanishing problem.
      }
      Sounds familiar? Martin Minow made the same observation in 1984.

      \Bquote{
        Plauger noted that no really good technical solution is likely to surface after so many years of study. The issue is now much more politics than technology - are we going to help Denmark find a palatable solution and will Denmark accept one without still more changes? If not, then WG14 must give up on consensus in this area. Otherwise, the entire normative addendum will be at risk.
      }

      \Bquote{
        Eventually, Plum suggested that we either drop the Danish proposal entirely or revert to the version already adopted by X3J16.
      }

      At the same meeting another revision of the digraph proposal is seen. In this version the following digraphs are proposed:
      \digraphs{
        \digraph("<:", "[")
        \digraph(":>", "]")
        \digraph("<%", "#")
        \digraph("<%<%", "##")
        \digraph("<>", "!=")
      }

      According to the minutes, several people immediately objected to the continued inclusion of \tcode{<>}. Also, the following comment was made:
      \Bquote{
        Weil observed that :> collides with a popular extension to Microsoft C.
      }

      Several polls were taken:
\codeblock("text"){
Straw vote:
  2 accept N218 with the two changes
 15 no

Straw vote:
  8 accept N222 with <> replaced by NE
  9 no

Straw vote:
  7 try to solve digraph problem some way
 12 no
}

At the same meeting it was once again clarified that the Danish C Panel insisted on reinstating \tcode{<>}. Several NBs expressed their positions:
\Bquote{Jones the Only said that the UK now favors macros, but no digraphs because they might change existing code.}
\Bquote{Jaeschke reported that the US now opposes any digraph solution, because the proposals keep changing.}
\Bquote{Noda said that Japan wants to support the Danish proposal from an international standpoint, but doesn't want to delay the normative addendum. He asked whether the proposal can be made optional instead of part of the C Standard. Simonsen replied that Denmark wants digraphs to be mandatory.}
\Bquote{Plauger suggested that Denmark make one more try to draft a proposal that can achieve consensus. If they fail to do so by the Dec '92 meeting, however, they must face the serious possibility that the normative addendum will be voted out for balloting without a digraph proposal.}
}

\timeline_event(
  date="November 2-6, 1992",
  title="X3J16/WG21 Meeting",
  source={\ref("WG21/N0206")}
){
  Problems with digraphs in keywords are encountered. To quote the minutes:
  \Bquote{
    Roskind objected to the proposal because allowing space between \tcode{new} and \tcode{[]} conflicts with existing practice. Gibbons explained that there are digraphs for \tcode{[} and \tcode{]} (\tcode{<:} and \tcode{:>} respectively) and the subgroup did not want keywords containing digraphs.
  }
}

\timeline_event(
    date="November 13, 1992",
