\h2{Design Space}
\h3{Splicers}

Splicers from \ref("P2996") were accepted for C++26 with the proposed syntax \tcode{[: expr :]}. However,
we are not allowed to use digraphs to spell this as \tcode{<:: expr ::>}.

While that seems to be in direct contradiction of the guarantees we're given in \eelis{lex.digraph#2}
\Bquote{
In all respects of the language, each alternative token behaves the same, respectively, as its primary token, except for its spelling.
}

it actually isn't. The tokens \tcode{[:} and \tcode{:]} are distinct preprocessing tokens rather than being 
composed from \tcode{[} and \tcode{:} (or \tcode{:} and \tcode{]} respectively). Therefore it doesn't matter whether \tcode{<:} is a valid
alternative spelling for \tcode{[} - the splicer syntax does not contain \tcode{[} tokens.

Unfortunately that doesn't exactly help if your source encoding does not have square brackets. In such
cases you cannot use this language feature directly - you'd have to find some workaround (such as 
inventing some arbitrary replacement sequence that is expanded to \tcode{[:} during or after transcoding).

\h3{Interpolated string literals}
The design problems stemming from digraphs do not end there. In some of the recent discussions around the proposed string interpolation feature (\ref("P3412"), \ref("P3951")) some interesting code was brought up. Consider the following:

\codeblock("cpp"){
t"foo \{ bar %> baz"
}

In an interpolated string literal, the interpolated expression field is wrapped in curly braces. To parse
an interpolated string literal you must therefore switch between regular string literal parsing and expression parsing as soon
as you see a field introducer (\tcode{\{}). 

However, once you parse the interpolated expression things get a little strange. 
\tcode{%>} is an alternative spelling of \tcode{\}}. We haven't yet returned back to literal parsing, so this would yield the correct token. So, should we be able to signify the end of the interpolation field with \tcode{%>}?

Since allowing anything but literal \tcode{\}} to terminate a interpolation field seems extremely surprising and will most likely not match user expectations, we are once again looking for a workaround because of digraphs.

To make matters worse, there is another ambiguity. Consider an interpolated literal:
\codeblock("cpp"){
t"foo \{ bar :>5\}"
}

In this example, is \tcode{:>} the digraph for \tcode{]} or is it the start of the format specifiers of that field? In a non-interpolated format literal, \tcode{"{:>5}"} would be a valid format specifier.


\h2{Affected encodings}

A common way to support arbitrary source encodings is by normalizing the source code through iconv. Therefore, we are typically limited by what iconv supports. Note that iconv also supports several translation character sets that are irrelevant for our purposes. The codec names in this chapter are the primary/canonical names used by iconv rather than necessarily actual codec names.

To figure out whether a particular encoding needs digraphs, we can check whether iconv fails when trying to convert the characters \tcode{#}, \tcode{[}, \tcode{]}, \tcode{\{} or \tcode{\}} to that encoding. If it does, then that encoding requires digraphs.

In a similar fashion, other characters that have meaning in C++ can be checked. For example, if an encoding does not support \tcode{:}, several digraphs are not usable either. As mentioned before, one such encoding is LATIN-GREEK-1 (\ref("ISO-IR-027")).

On a somewhat recent Arch Linux system, this gives us the following support matrix: \ref("https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/10GTvVdC4mMrzSERlNCYJjwx2Ml85IJ9RYXDu9HmNbYo/edit?usp=sharing"){View on Google Sheets}

Out of 279 considered encodings, 200 can encode the everything in the basic character set. However, 16 cannot even encode at least one character in the ranges \tcode{a-z}, \tcode{A-Z} or \tcode{0-9}. These are mostly replacement character sets and special purpose encodings (e.g. for Braille or OCR purposes) and are therefore disregarded for the purposes of this document.

\p{
Out of the remaining 263 considered encodings, the following \b{49 encodings} lack at least one character for which we have a digraph:
}

\compact_list{
\li{ISO646-CA}
\li{ISO646-CA2}
\li{ISO646-CU}
\li{ISO646-DE}
\li{ISO646-DK}
\li{ISO646-ES}
\li{ISO646-ES2}
\li{ISO646-FI}
\li{ISO646-FR}
\li{ISO646-FR1}
\li{ISO646-GB}
\li{ISO646-HU}
\li{ISO646-IT}
\li{ISO646-JP-OCR-B}
\li{ISO646-NO2}
\li{ISO646-NO}
\li{ISO646-PT}
\li{ISO646-PT2}
\li{ISO646-SE2}
\li{ISO646-YU}

\li{EBCDIC-AT-DE}
\li{EBCDIC-AT-DE-A}
\li{EBCDIC-CA-FR}
\li{EBCDIC-DK-NO}
\li{EBCDIC-DK-NO-A}
\li{EBCDIC-ES-A}
\li{EBCDIC-ES}
\li{EBCDIC-ES-S}
\li{EBCDIC-FI-SE}
\li{EBCDIC-FI-SE-A}
\li{EBCDIC-FR}
\li{EBCDIC-IS-FRISS}
\li{EBCDIC-IT}
\li{EBCDIC-UK}
\li{EBCDIC-US}
\li{EBCDIC-PT}

\li{IBM274}
\li{IBM275}
\li{IBM281}
\li{IBM420}
\li{IBM423}
\li{IBM880}
\li{IBM4517}
\li{IBM16804}

\li{NATS-DANO}
\li{NATS-SEFI}
\li{Latin-greek-1}
\li{INIS}
\li{T.61-8bit}
}

\p{
While these are not the only encodings that cannot represent \tcode{\\} directly, the following \b{40 encodings} out of those 49 also cannot represent \tcode{\\} directly:
}
\compact_list{
\li{ISO646-CA}
\li{ISO646-CA2}
\li{ISO646-CU}
\li{ISO646-DE}
\li{ISO646-DK}
\li{ISO646-ES}
\li{ISO646-ES2}
\li{ISO646-FI}
\li{ISO646-FR}
\li{ISO646-FR1}
\li{ISO646-HU}
\li{ISO646-IT}
\li{ISO646-JP-OCR-B}
\li{ISO646-NO2}
\li{ISO646-NO}
\li{ISO646-PT}
\li{ISO646-PT2}
\li{ISO646-SE2}
\li{ISO646-YU}
\li{EBCDIC-AT-DE}
\li{EBCDIC-CA-FR}
\li{EBCDIC-DK-NO-A}
\li{EBCDIC-ES-A}
\li{EBCDIC-FI-SE}
\li{EBCDIC-FI-SE-A}
\li{EBCDIC-FR}
\li{EBCDIC-IS-FRISS}
\li{EBCDIC-IT}
\li{EBCDIC-PT}
\li{IBM274}
\li{IBM281}
\li{IBM420}
\li{IBM423}
\li{IBM4517}
\li{IBM16804}
\li{NATS-DANO}
\li{NATS-SEFI}
\li{Latin-greek-1}
\li{INIS}
\li{T.61-8bit}
}

For those, digraphs help but do not provide a complete solution either.


\p{
We can narrow that list down even further. The most interesting digraph is \tcode{%:}, since it can be used to spell \tcode{#pragma}. 
Some compilers already have pragmas to specify the source encoding - for example, the IBM z/OS XL compilers support \tcode{#pragma filetag} (\ref("https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos/3.2.0?topic=descriptions-pragma-filetag"){documentation}) for this purpose.
}

\p{
If we only have to retain a digraph for \tcode{#}, this narrows the 49 plausible encodings down to the following \b{22}:
}

\compact_list{
  \li{EBCDIC-AT-DE-A}
  \li{EBCDIC-DK-NO-A}
  \li{EBCDIC-ES}
  \li{EBCDIC-ES-A}
  \li{EBCDIC-ES-S}
  \li{EBCDIC-FI-SE}
  \li{EBCDIC-FI-SE-A}
  \li{EBCDIC-FR}
  \li{EBCDIC-IT}
  \li{EBCDIC-PT}
  \li{ISO646-ES}
  \li{ISO646-FR}
  \li{ISO646-FR1}
  \li{ISO646-GB}
  \li{ISO646-IT}
  \li{ISO646-NO2}
  \li{IBM275}
  \li{IBM423}
  \li{IBM4517}
  \li{NATS-DANO}
  \li{Latin-greek-1}
  \li{INIS}
}

Since some compilers support pragmas such as \tcode{#pragma filetag("...")}, these encodings are especially interesting. For these encodings, digraphs allow us to write code in an encoding-agnostic way by using \tcode{%:pragma filetag("...")} instead of \tcode{#pragma filetag("...")}.
This is especially useful for shared headers.

\p{
  Out of the 49 plausible encodings, only \b{8} have replacements for all characters that currently have meaning in C++. This includes the alternative operators \tcode{and}, \tcode{or} and so on.
}
\compact_list{
  \li{ISO646-GB}
  \li{IBM275}
  \li{IBM880}
  \li{EBCDIC-UK}
  \li{EBCDIC-US}
  \li{EBCDIC-ES}
  \li{EBCDIC-ES-S}
  \li{EBCDIC-DK-NO}
}


While this isn't a huge list, it proves the point that there are still encodings that require digraphs.

However, the question remains whether there is any actual use of any of these in practice. 
Fortunately those are all rather obscure encodings by today's standards.

\h2{Usage Analysis}
So, are people still using digraphs? Are they targeting somewhat modern C++ versions?

A GitHub code search can unfortunately not answer this question for us, since a lot of use might be in code that isn't publicly available. However, it helps getting a rough idea of the situation and how people use digraphs.

Surprisingly, we can actually find quite a lot of code that uses digraphs. For example \tcode{%:include} is included in \ref("https://github.com/search?q=%25%3Ainclude+NOT+is%3Afork+language%3AC%2B%2B&type=code&l=C%2B%2B"){850 files}!

However, if you take a closer look at those results, you'll quickly notice that aside from a bunch of false positives most of them fall into one of three categories:
\ol{
\li{Compiler test code and other lexer test code}
\li{Demo code, examples and exercises}
\li{Inconsistent use of digraphs and the tokens they replace, possibly for obfuscation purposes}
}

Note how this list is missing "actual production code that uses digraphs for anything other than tests".

For the other digraphs the situation is similar. \tcode{%:%:} yields \ref("https://github.com/search?q=%25%3A%25%3A+NOT+is%3Afork+language%3AC%2B%2B&type=code&p=1"){216 results}, most of which are false positives and/or lexer code. \tcode{%:if} yields \ref("https://github.com/search?q=%25%3Aif+NOT+is%3Afork+language%3AC%2B%2B&type=code"){22 results}, all of which are university assignments, inconsistent or false positives. 

For the promising \tcode{%:pragma} we get \ref("https://github.com/search?q=%25%3Apragma+NOT+is%3Afork+language%3AC%2B%2B&type=code&p=1"){107 results}, most if not all of which are inconsistent. For \tcode{%:pragma filetag} we get \ref("https://github.com/search?q=%25%3Apragma+filetag+NOT+is%3Afork&type=code"){0 results} (even for the trigraph spelling we at least get \ref("https://github.com/search?q=%3F%3F%3Dpragma+filetag+NOT+is%3Afork&type=code"){106 results}).

For the digraphs \tcode{<:}, \tcode{:>}, \tcode{<%} and \tcode{%>} a code search yields \b{a lot} more results, but they seem to be almost exclusively false positives - usually because they appear in string literals or comments. Furthermore, \tcode{<:} yields especially bad results due to qualified template arguments being spelled as \tcode{templ<::arg>} (without space after <).

\h2{Compatibility}
At the time of writing almost all major compilers still support trigraphs as an extension. For instance:
\ul{
\li{GCC has \ref("https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Preprocessor-Options.html#index-trigraphs"){\tcode{-trigraphs}}}
\li{Clang has \ref("https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangCommandLineReference.html#cmdoption-clang-ftrigraphs"){\tcode{-ftrigraphs}}}
\li{MSVC has \ref("https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/build/reference/zc-trigraphs-trigraphs-substitution?view=msvc-170"){\tcode{/Zc:trigraphs}}}
\li{EDG has \ref("https://edg.com/docs/edg_cpp.pdf"){\tcode{--trigraphs}}}
}

Some compilers already support disabling digraph support altogether. For instance:
\ul{
\li{Clang has \ref("https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangCommandLineReference.html#cmdoption-clang-fdigraphs"){\tcode{-fno-digraphs}}}
\li{EDG has \ref("https://edg.com/docs/edg_cpp.pdf"){\tcode{--no_alternative_tokens}} (which also disables the alternative operators \tcode{and}, \tcode{or} etc.)}
\li{MSVC's documentation of compiler warning \ref("https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/error-messages/compiler-warnings/compiler-warning-level-1-c4628?view=msvc-170"){C4628} and \ref("https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/error-messages/compiler-warnings/compiler-warning-level-4-c4629?view=msvc-170"){C4629} suggests using digraphs is not supported with \tcode{/Ze} and will cause a warning with \tcode{/Za}, however \ref("https://compiler-explorer.com/z/P43W48EKW"){Compiler Explorer} does not verify that}
}

\h2{Alternatives to full digraph removal}

As mentioned before, compilers that have pragmas such as \tcode{#pragma filetag("encoding")} only really need some way of spelling that.

In \ref("WG14/N3116") Rajan Bhakta confirms this:
\Bquote{
  Theoretically the \tcode{#} is the only one we need, to engage the \tcode{pragma}.
}

Since there are several ways to spell \tcode{#pragma}, there are multiple avenues to explore. 
The \ref("https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/10GTvVdC4mMrzSERlNCYJjwx2Ml85IJ9RYXDu9HmNbYo/edit?usp=sharing"){Google Sheet} from \ref("#affected-encodings") compares the different spellings across encodings in the "Pragma Analysis" sheet

\h3{Use \tcode{_Pragma("...")} where available}

In C99 and C++11 \tcode{_Pragma} was standardized as an alternative to \tcode{#pragma}.
With this spelling \tcode{#} is not directly required. 

However, if the pragma's argument is a string literal, the \tcode{#} or \tcode{\\} characters are still required to escape the argument properly. 
Unfortunately that is the case for the \tcode{filetag} pragma, therefore this is not a complete solution either.

As demonstrated in the "Pragma Analysis" sheet, this is not a purely theoretical concern.
For example, the following encodings can represent both \tcode{??=pragma filetag("IBM-1047")} and \tcode{%:pragma filetag("IBM-1047")}, but neither one of \tcode{_Pragma("filetag(\\\"IBM-1047\\\")")} and \tcode{#pragma filetag("IBM-1047")}:

\compact_list{
  \li{EBCDIC-FI-SE}
  \li{EBCDIC-FR}
  \li{EBCDIC-IT}
  \li{EBCDIC-PT}
  \li{ISO646-ES}
  \li{ISO646-FR}
  \li{ISO646-FR1}
  \li{ISO646-IT}
  \li{ISO646-NO2}
  \li{IBM4517}
  \li{IBM423}
}


\h3{Partially remove digraphs}
Instead of removing all digraphs, we could remove all digraphs except for \tcode{%:} (and possibly \tcode{%:%:}). This would allow users to spell \tcode{#pragma} as \tcode{%:pragma}.

This option seems feasible since \tcode{%:} is relatively harmless - it does not conflict with any other language features in the way \tcode{<:} or \tcode{%>} do and we could still get rid of those.

However, it's not clear whether a tiny amount of actual usage of this feature warrants keeping it around for another couple of decades, especially given that there are other alternatives. 

Furthermore, the usage analysis in \ref("#usage-analysis") shows that being able to spell \tcode{%:pragma filetag} is largely a theoretical concern as compilers still have opt-in support for trigraphs.


\h3{Partially reintroduce trigraphs}

Since most if not all code that actually uses \tcode{#pragma filetag} with an encoding that cannot represent \tcode{#} uses the trigraph spelling rather than the digraph spelling, we could also reintroduce just the \tcode{??=} trigraph. This would allow users to once again spell \tcode{#pragma filetag} as \tcode{??=pragma filetag}.

As with the other options, this mostly is up to whether the C and C++ committees feel like supporting such encodings is worth the effort. 

It also seems rather unlikely that either language is going to reuse \tcode{??=} for any other purposes anytime soon, meaning that this is a conforming extension either way. If compilers want to keep supporting this, they absolutely can.

\h3{Introduce another alternative spelling for \tcode{#pragma}}

Instead of keeping \tcode{%:} around or reintroducing \tcode{??=}, it might also be viable to just introduce another spelling for \tcode{#pragma} that does not require stringifaction of the arguments.

Particularly interesting for this is that \tcode{??=} may still be supported as a conforming extension. 
Therefore, implementations may already conformingly parse \tcode{??=pragma} as a single token representing a valid \tcode{#pragma} directive, regardless of whether they support trigraphs fully or not.

However, since such pragmas are often wrapped in macro guards, the same may have to be applied to other preprocessor diretives as well.

Since this does not necessarily require any changes to the standard (as it is doable as conforming extension), this is not pursued at this point.

\h3{Deprecate digraphs first}

It seems highly unlikely that deprecation is actually meaningful in this case.

Users that still rely on encodings that require digraphs are likely not going to be able to update their code regardless of deprecation warnings or not. This would just delay removal and is not pursued at this point.

\h2{Wording}

Make the following changes to the C++ Working Draft.  All wording is relative
to \ref("N5054"), the latest draft at the time of writing.
\cowel_html_element("h2"){Lexical conventions  \stable_ref{lex}}
\cowel_html_element("h3"){Preprocessing tokens \stable_ref{lex.pptoken}}
Modify paragraph 5 as indicated

\Bdiff{
5
If the input stream has been parsed into preprocessing tokens up to a given character:

5.1
    If the next character begins a sequence of characters that could be the prefix and initial double quote of a raw string literal, such as `R"`, the next preprocessing token shall be a raw string literal.
    Between the initial and final double quote characters of the raw string, any transformations performed in phase 2 (line splicing) are reverted; this reversion is applied before any \grammarterm{d-char}, \grammarterm{r-char}, or delimiting parenthesis is identified.
    The raw string literal is defined as the shortest sequence of characters that matches the raw-string pattern

    \grammarterm{encoding-prefix}\sub{opt} \tcode{R} \grammarterm{raw-string}

\del{
5.2
    Otherwise, if the next three characters are \tcode{<::} and the subsequent character is neither \tcode{:} nor \tcode{>}, the \tcode{<} is treated as a preprocessing token by itself and not as the first character of the alternative token \tcode{<:}.
}

5.3
    Otherwise, if the next three characters are \tcode{[::} and the subsequent character is not \tcode{:}\del{, or if the next three characters are \tcode{:>},} the \tcode{[} is treated as a preprocessing token by itself and not as the first character of the preprocessing token \tcode{[:}.

\del{
  \note{
    The tokens \tcode{[:} and \tcode{:]} cannot be composed from digraphs.
  }
}

5.4
    Otherwise, the next preprocessing token is the longest sequence of [...]
}

\cowel_html_element("h3"){Operators and punctuators \stable_ref{lex.operators}}
Modify as indicated.

\Bdiff{
1
The lexical representation of C++ programs includes a number of preprocessing tokens that are used in the syntax of the preprocessor or are converted into tokens for operators and punctuators:


\dt{preprocessing-op-or-punc:}
\dd{preprocessing-operator}
\dd{operator-or-punctuator}

\dt{preprocessing-operator:}
\dd{one of}
\dd{
\table(style="border: none; --border: none;"){
\tr{\td{\tcode{#}} \td{\tcode{##}} \td{\del{\tcode{%:}}} \td{\del{\tcode{%:%:}}}}
}
}

\dt{operator-or-punctuator:}
\dd{one of}
\dd{\table(style="border: none; --border: none;"){
\tr{\td{\tcode{\{}}         \td{\tcode{\}}}         \td{\tcode{[}}          \td{\tcode{]}}          \td{\tcode{(}}        \td{\tcode{)}}       \td{\tcode{[:}}      \td{\tcode{:]}}}
\tr{\td{\del{\tcode{<%}}}   \td{\del{\tcode{%>}}}   \td{\del{\tcode{<:}}}   \td{\del{\tcode{:>}}}   \td{\tcode{;}}        \td{\tcode{:}}       \td{\tcode{...}}}
\tr{\td{\tcode{?}}          \td{\tcode{::}}         \td{\tcode{.}}          \td{\tcode{.*}}         \td{\tcode{->}}       \td{\tcode{->*}}     \td{\tcode{^^}}      \td{\tcode{~}}}
\tr{\td{\tcode{!}}          \td{\tcode{+}}          \td{\tcode{-}}          \td{\tcode{*}}          \td{\tcode{/}}        \td{\tcode{%}}       \td{\tcode{^}}       \td{\tcode{&}}    \td{\tcode{|}}  }
\tr{\td{\tcode{=}}          \td{\tcode{+=}}         \td{\tcode{-=}}         \td{\tcode{*=}}         \td{\tcode{/=}}       \td{\tcode{%=}}      \td{\tcode{^=}}      \td{\tcode{&=}}   \td{\tcode{|=}} }
\tr{\td{\tcode{==}}         \td{\tcode{!=}}         \td{\tcode{<}}          \td{\tcode{>}}          \td{\tcode{<=}}       \td{\tcode{>=}}      \td{\tcode{<=>}}     \td{\tcode{&&}}   \td{\tcode{||}} }
\tr{\td{\tcode{<<}}         \td{\tcode{>>}}         \td{\tcode{<<=}}        \td{\tcode{>>=}}        \td{\tcode{++}}       \td{\tcode{--}}      \td{\tcode{,}}}
\tr{\td{\tcode{and}}        \td{\tcode{or}}         \td{\tcode{xor}}        \td{\tcode{not}}        \td{\tcode{bitand}}   \td{\tcode{bitor}}   \td{\tcode{compl}}}
\tr{\td{\tcode{and_eq}}     \td{\tcode{or_eq}}      \td{\tcode{xor_eq}}     \td{\tcode{not_eq}}}
}}


Each \grammarterm{operator-or-punctuator} is converted to a single token in translation phase 6 (\eelis{lex.phases}).
}


\cowel_html_element("h3"){Alternative tokens \stable_ref{lex.\del{digraph}\ins{alt}}}
Rename to [lex.alt] and update all references accordingly.

\Bdiff{
1 Alternative token representations are provided for some operators\del{ and punctuators}.

2 In all respects of the language, each alternative token behaves the same, respectively, as its primary token, except for its spelling.

\del{
\note{
  The “stringized” values (\eelis{cpp.stringize}) of \tcode{[} and \tcode{<:} are different, maintaining the source spelling.
}
}

The set of alternative tokens is defined in Table 3.
}

Modify Table 3
\Bdiff{
\table{
\tr{\td{\del{Alternative}}  \td{\del{Primary}}    \td{Alternative} \td{Primary} \td{Alternative} \td{Primary}}

\tr{\td{\del{\tcode{<%}}}   \td{\del{\tcode{\{}}} \td{\tcode{and}}        \td{\tcode{&&}}    \td{\tcode{and_eq}}    \td{\tcode{&=}}}
\tr{\td{\del{\tcode{%>}}}   \td{\del{\tcode{\}}}} \td{\tcode{bitor}}      \td{\tcode{|}}     \td{\tcode{or_eq}}     \td{\tcode{|=}}}
